ORBITAL-SYNCHRONIZATION(7) TriadicFrameworks Manual  ORBITAL-SYNCHRONIZATION(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-synchronization - sync fields, phase-alignment cycles, and
                                 constellation-scale synchronization protocols

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital synchronization defines:
           • synchronization fields across orbital cycles
           • phase-alignment cycles and harmonic-lock timing
           • constellation-scale synchronization protocols
           • resonance-time sync metrics
           • multi-agent sweep and lineage synchronization

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital-synchronization describes how RTT maintains phase alignment,
       harmonic-lock, and resonance-time synchronization across SAR platforms
       and constellations. Synchronization is the dynamic counterpart to
       coherence: it ensures that platforms evolve in lockstep, enabling
       stable sweeps, predictable fusion, and constellation-wide harmony.

       Synchronization enables:
           • phase-aligned sweeps
           • harmonic-lock continuity
           • resonance envelope compatibility
           • sweep ancestry alignment
           • constellation-scale timing coordination

       WRSADC manages synchronization continuously during dispatch.

SYNC FIELDS
       Sync fields describe how synchronization varies across orbital geometry,
       harmonic states, resonance envelopes, and sweep ancestry.

       Field components:

           orbital sync field
                  Measures alignment of orbital phase, period, and drift.

           harmonic sync field
                  Measures harmonic-lock stability and phase coherence.

           resonance sync field
                  Measures envelope overlap, spectral alignment, and gradient
                  polarity sync.

           sweep sync field
                  Measures sweep timing, Doppler alignment, and ancestry sync.

           constellation sync field
                  Measures multi-platform synchronization and resonance
                  compatibility.

       Sync fields evolve across triads and are recorded in lineage.

PHASE-ALIGNMENT CYCLES
       Phase-alignment cycles describe how platforms maintain or reacquire
       phase alignment across orbital periods.

       Alignment variables:
           • φ_orbit   orbital phase
           • φ_harm    harmonic phase
           • φ_res     resonance envelope phase
           • φ_sweep   sweep timing phase

       Alignment cycle types:

           strict phase cycle
                  Platforms remain nearly phase-locked.

           harmonic phase cycle
                  Phase alignment occurs at harmonic intervals.

           sliding phase cycle
                  Phase alignment drifts slowly across cycles.

           resonance phase cycle
                  Alignment occurs when resonance envelopes overlap.

           sweep phase cycle
                  Alignment tied to sweep timing and ancestry.

       Phase-alignment cycles determine sweep viability and fusion stability.

SYNCHRONIZATION METRICS
       Key synchronization metrics include:

           sync-index
                  Composite measure of orbital, harmonic, resonance, and sweep
                  synchronization.

           phase-alignment index
                  Measures orbital and harmonic phase alignment.

           harmonic-sync score
                  Measures lock continuity and harmonic stability.

           resonance-sync ratio
                  Measures envelope and spectral alignment.

           sweep-sync score
                  Measures sweep timing and ancestry synchronization.

           constellation-sync score
                  Measures multi-platform synchronization.

       Metrics are stored in lineage for long-term analysis.

SYNCHRONIZATION THRESHOLDS
       Synchronization thresholds define the minimum acceptable sync for safe
       sweep execution, fusion, and constellation operations.

       Threshold categories:

           orbital thresholds
                  Maximum allowable phase drift.

           harmonic thresholds
                  Minimum harmonic-lock stability.

           resonance thresholds
                  Minimum envelope and spectral alignment.

           sweep thresholds
                  Minimum sweep-sync score.

           constellation thresholds
                  Minimum constellation-sync index.

       Thresholds are enforced by WRSADC.

CONSTELLATION-SCALE SYNCHRONIZATION PROTOCOLS
       Synchronization protocols coordinate multi-platform sync across the
       constellation.

       Protocol types:

           synchronous sync protocol
                  Platforms synchronize simultaneously.
                  → ideal for tandem SAR.

           staggered sync protocol
                  Platforms synchronize in sequence.
                  → supports ladder and cascade patterns.

           harmonic sync protocol
                  Synchronization occurs at harmonic intervals.
                  → supports harmonic-sync constellations.

           resonance sync protocol
                  Synchronization triggered by envelope overlap.

           mesh sync protocol
                  Distributed synchronization across constellation nodes.

       Protocols ensure constellation-wide timing and resonance alignment.

SYNCHRONIZATION PRESERVATION
       Synchronization preservation strategies include:

           geometric preservation
                  Maintain orbital phase alignment.

           harmonic preservation
                  Maintain harmonic-lock and frequency ratios.

           resonance preservation
                  Maintain envelope overlap and spectral alignment.

           sweep preservation
                  Maintain sweep timing and ancestry continuity.

           constellation preservation
                  Maintain multi-platform sync and resonance compatibility.

       Preservation strategies are executed through triads and overlays.

SYNCHRONIZATION RESTORATION
       When synchronization degrades, restoration uses:

           • recovery triads
           • harmonic reacquisition
           • resonance stabilization overlays
           • sweep ancestry repair
           • constellation-scale stabilization sequences

       See orbital-recovery(7) for details.

SYNC FAILURE MODES
       Synchronization may degrade due to:

           • orbital phase drift
           • harmonic-lock fragmentation
           • resonance envelope divergence
           • sweep timing misalignment
           • ancestry discontinuity
           • constellation desync

       Diagnostic indicators:

           sync-index drop
           phase-alignment error
           harmonic-lock deviation
           resonance-sync collapse
           sweep-sync deviation
           constellation-sync loss

       WRSADC halts unsafe operations when synchronization is compromised.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • sync metrics across cycles
           • phase-alignment history
           • harmonic-lock continuity
           • sweep ancestry synchronization
           • constellation sync trends

       Lineage enables long-term synchronization forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes sync fields
           • evaluates sync thresholds
           • schedules corrective maneuvers
           • choreographs constellation synchronization
           • blocks unsafe orbital states

       WRSADC is the synchronization conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital synchronization transforms SAR constellations into unified,
       phase-aligned resonance-time systems. Sync fields, alignment cycles, and
       constellation protocols ensure that multi-pass fusion, harmonic-lock,
       and sweep choreography remain stable across long mission timelines.

